.TH HOCTEST 1
.SH NAME
hoctest \- regression test for hoc(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hoctest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Hoctest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/hoc.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(argv) ,
and checks it against a real, live oracle: plan9port's own
.B hoc
binary, run through this emu's host-command-execution device
.RB ( #C ,
see
.IR cmd (3)),
on the identical file this same case wrote under
.BR /hoctest ,
with the identical arguments. Adapted directly from
.IR prtest (1)'s
own
.BR runhost() / hostroot() ,
except stdout and stderr come back as two SEPARATE buffers rather than
one concatenated one, since several cases here compare stdout exactly
while only checking that stderr reported SOMETHING (hoc's own
diagnostics embed a file path and line number that differ between an
Inferno path and a host path, the same way
.IR pr (1)'s
own page-header date differs - not worth reproducing exactly for
every error case here).
.PP
Most cases compare stdout byte-exact. A few (constants, a
transcendental builtin) compare through a numeric-tolerance check
instead: every printed number here uses
.IR hoc (1)'s
own
.B "%.17g"
verbatim, but the real oracle's
.B print()
appears to print the shortest decimal that still round-trips, capped
at 17 significant digits, not always exactly 17 - so an irrational
value's last digit or two may legitimately differ between the two
sides without either being wrong (see
.IR hoc (1)'s
own BUGS). The tolerance check splits both outputs into whitespace-
separated tokens and requires either an exact match or, for a token
that parses as a bare number on both sides, a relative difference
under 1e-13; anything that doesn't parse as a number falls back to
requiring an exact match.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/hoctest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR hoc (1)
.SH BUGS
.B T5
and
.B T5b
exercise the real, oracle-confirmed quirk that
.BR read() 's
own leading-whitespace skip never skips a newline, so a loop reading
one newline-separated value per line only ever completes its first
iteration before hitting what looks like a non-numeric character (see
.IR hoc (1)'s
own BUGS) - genuine behavior, not a bug in either binary, but easy to
mistake for one when writing a hoc script by hand.
.PP
Does not exercise multi-line function/procedure bodies more elaborate
than the recursion/procedure-call cases already here, nested
control-flow combinations beyond what T3/T5 already need, or the
.RB ( "-e" )
flag combined with a syntax error partway through a multi-statement
inline expression.
